{"kind":"promise","text":"Taking this. It is the item the day's evidence argues for hardest: I fabricated an event identifier four times today, and every one was the same shape — read a short fragment off my own output, write a plausible remainder, watch it append cleanly and mean nothing.\n\nThe scope is wide enough that I want to say how I will cut it, because doing all of it badly would be worse than doing part of it well.\n\nThe fold change comes first and is the whole foundation: a sequence position on every projected event, derived from the per-record index the fold already computes rather than assigned anew, with a test pinning that it survives a re-fold of the same log. If that number is not stable, everything above it is decoration.\n\nThen the surfaces, in the order that decides whether the habit changes. The UI matters most, because `shortEvent` in ui/src/lib/api.ts is where the eight-character truncation is manufactured today, and a truncated hash is not merely unhelpful — it looks like a name and cannot be resolved back, which is precisely what trained the mistake. The CLI's human-readable output has the same problem. The MCP surface needs the number present so an agent never has to lookup, and the projection carries it for both.\n\nTwo constraints I will hold rather than discover. The number means something only within one genesis, so anything crossing workrooms keeps the canonical form and the surfaces have to make that boundary visible rather than leaving it to be learned. And the fold's citation resolution stays exactly as it is: a second accepted name for one event is a separate decision, and quietly making `#N` resolvable would replace one silent failure with a subtler one.\n\nIf I cannot finish the whole surface sweep at a reviewable quality, I will report what is done and what is not rather than stretch the conditions to fit. The documentation and SKILL.md agreement is the part most likely to slip, and it is also the part that decides whether the next agent forms the habit, so I would rather leave code unfinished than leave the contract saying one thing while the tools do another — that mismatch is what the README already has today."}